<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Second East Turkestan Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1944 CE – 1949 CE · capital Ghulja</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Soviet-backed republic in the Ili region of northern Xinjiang, founded during the Ili Rebellion of 1944 and absorbed into the People's Republic in 1949.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1944 resentment against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a> governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheng-shicai">Sheng Shicai</a> and Nationalist rule, encouraged by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, erupted in the Ili Rebellion. Rebels took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yining">Ghulja</a> (Yining), and on 12 November 1944 proclaimed the East Turkestan Republic, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> cleric <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elihan-tore">Elihan Töre</a> as president.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>With Soviet weapons and advisers, the republic built an army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhs">Kazakhs</a> and other peoples and held the three northern districts of Ili, Tarbagatai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai</a>. In 1946 it joined a coalition provincial government with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nationalist-government">Nationalist government</a>, negotiated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-zhizhong">Zhang Zhizhong</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elihan-tore">Elihan Töre</a> was removed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, and the secular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmetjan-qasimi">Ahmetjan Qasimi</a> led the region.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In August 1949 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmetjan-qasimi">Ahmetjan Qasimi</a> and other leaders died in a plane crash on their way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>. The remaining leadership, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saifuddin-azizi">Saifuddin Azizi</a>, accepted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-liberation-army">People's Liberation Army</a>, and the region became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/second-east-turkestan-republic.jpg" alt="Second East Turkestan Republic" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Unknown original designer; commissioned by the government of the (Second) East Turkestan Republic. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_East_Turkestan_Republic_(Seypidin).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1944 CE – 1949 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ghulja</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Elihan Töre, Ahmetjan Qasimi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Uyghur, Kazakh</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>East Turkestan Republic (1944-1949), Ili Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1946&amp;focus=second-east-turkestan-republic" aria-label="Second East Turkestan Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6631 622 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Second East Turkestan Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6951 878L8221 1549M6951 878L7868 1006"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8221 1549h0M7868 1006h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6951 878h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6951 878h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1946&amp;focus=second-east-turkestan-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
